# Turns the version requirement in package.json from a note into a refusal.
#
# package-lock.json is written by npm, and npm versions do not all write it the same way: an npm
# older than 11.19 does not know the `libc` field that tells a musl build apart from a glibc one, so
# it silently drops it from every Linux binary entry on any install. That is not a broken lockfile —
# it installs fine — which is exactly why it went unnoticed until 30 unrelated lines rode into a
# feature commit, and the image then installed both binaries instead of the one it needs.
#
# ⚠️ This flag is broader than the reason it was added for: it enforces the engines range of EVERY
# dependency, not just this project's. That is why the node floor is 24.15 rather than 24 — jsdom
# asks for ^24.15.0, so anything lower fails the install naming jsdom, which reads as somebody
# else's problem. Checked against the whole tree: no dependency currently refuses node 24.20 or 26.
# When one eventually does, it fails loudly at install with the package named, which is the failure
# this repo prefers over a silent rewrite.
#
# CI and the production image both run npm 11.19.0 already, so this costs them nothing and only ever
# fires on a machine that would have rewritten the lockfile behind your back.
engine-strict=true
